delete from public.reading_texts where title_en in ('The Night Market', 'Learning to Cook at Thirty', 'Why Songs Get Stuck in Our Heads', 'A Shorter Week?', 'The Hidden Cost of Convenience', 'What Libraries Are For Now', 'On Being Wrong', 'The Map Is Not the Territory');
delete from public.listening_clips where title_en in ('A Voice Message from the Landlord', 'Booking a Doctor''s Appointment', 'Returning a Faulty Laptop', 'A Podcast Opening: Screens and Sleep', 'A Team Meeting About a Delay', 'A Museum Audio Guide', 'A Radio Essay on Silence', 'A Debate on Learning Online');

insert into public.reading_texts (title_he, title_en, body_en, cefr_level, sort_order) values
  ('השוק הלילי', 'The Night Market', 'On my second evening in Taipei, my friend Dana suggested we visit a night market. I imagined a few stalls selling snacks, so I was surprised when we turned a corner and saw a street full of lights, smoke and people. The smell of grilled corn and sweet tea was everywhere.

Dana wanted to try everything, and I followed her from stall to stall. We ate small pancakes filled with green onion, a soup with tiny dumplings, and a strange dessert made of ice and beans that I did not expect to like but finished completely. Most sellers spoke very little English, but pointing and smiling worked surprisingly well.

After about an hour, I stopped to take a photo of a man making noodles by hand. When I turned around, Dana was gone. The crowd was so thick that I could not see more than a few metres. My phone battery was almost empty, and I did not remember the name of our hotel, only that it was near a park.

I was starting to panic when an older woman selling fruit asked me, in careful English, if I was lost. I explained that I was looking for my friend. She did not have a phone to lend me, but she gave me a slice of mango, told me to stay next to her stall, and said that people who get lost here always come back to the place where they last saw each other.

She was right. Ten minutes later, Dana appeared, out of breath and laughing. She had been looking for me at the noodle stall. Before we left, I bought a whole bag of mangoes from the woman. Since then, whenever I travel, I write the name and address of my hotel on a piece of paper and keep it in my pocket.', 'B1', 100),
  ('ללמוד לבשל בגיל שלושים', 'Learning to Cook at Thirty', 'Until last year, my idea of cooking was boiling water for instant noodles. I am thirty, I have a job I like and an apartment with a decent kitchen, but for years that kitchen was mostly a place to keep takeaway menus.

Things changed when my doctor told me, kindly but clearly, that I was eating too much salt and too few vegetables. I left her office feeling a little embarrassed. That weekend, I bought a basic cookbook and decided to learn one new recipe every week.

The first weeks were not pretty. I burned rice, I cut my finger while slicing an onion, and I once added a tablespoon of salt instead of a teaspoon. But I noticed that each mistake taught me something specific. Rice needs less heat than I thought. A sharp knife is actually safer than a dull one. And reading the whole recipe before starting saves a lot of trouble.

By the third month, I could make five or six simple dishes without looking at the book. I started inviting friends for dinner, which was nervous at first but quickly became the best part of my week. People who never called me suddenly asked when the next dinner was.

The biggest surprise was not the food. It was the calm I felt while cooking. For forty minutes, I was not checking my phone or thinking about work. I was just chopping, stirring and tasting. I still order takeaway sometimes, but now it is a choice, not the only option I have.', 'B1', 101),
  ('למה שירים נתקעים לנו בראש', 'Why Songs Get Stuck in Our Heads', 'Almost everyone knows the experience. You hear a few seconds of a song in a shop, and for the rest of the day the same chorus plays in your head, again and again, whether you like the song or not. The experience is so common that it has an informal name: an "earworm".

Earworms seem to have a few things in common. They are usually short pieces of a song rather than the whole thing, often the chorus. They tend to be simple and repetitive, with a melody that rises and falls in a way that is easy to sing. And they are often songs we have heard many times recently, which suggests that repetition makes a tune more likely to return.

Interestingly, earworms often appear when the mind is not busy. Many people notice them while walking, showering or doing a routine task. One explanation is that the brain, when it is not focused on anything demanding, tends to replay recent and familiar material. A catchy chorus is perfect for this: it is familiar, it is short, and it feels unfinished, so the mind keeps returning to it.

That unfinished feeling may also explain one of the most popular pieces of advice for getting rid of an earworm: listen to the whole song from beginning to end. Some people find that completing the song helps the loop stop. Others prefer to distract themselves with a task that needs real attention, such as a difficult puzzle or a conversation. Chewing gum is sometimes suggested too, perhaps because it interferes with the small movements of the mouth that we make when we silently "sing" along.

For most people, earworms are a harmless and even slightly funny part of daily life. They are a reminder of how deeply music connects with memory, and of how much of our thinking happens without us deciding to do it.', 'B2', 102),
  ('שבוע עבודה קצר יותר?', 'A Shorter Week?', 'The idea of a four-day working week, with the same pay as five, used to sound like a fantasy. Today it is discussed seriously by managers, unions and governments, and some companies have tried it. The arguments on both sides are worth looking at carefully, because the answer probably depends on the kind of work involved.

Supporters start from a simple observation: many office workers do not spend all five days doing focused work. Hours disappear into long meetings, interruptions and messages that could have waited. If a team agrees to cut unnecessary meetings and protect time for concentration, they argue, it may be possible to produce the same results in four days. A free third day of the weekend could then mean better rest, more time with family and fewer people burning out.

Critics raise practical problems. A hospital, a school or a bus company cannot simply close on Fridays; someone still has to be there. For these jobs, a four-day week either means hiring more staff, which costs money, or asking people to work longer days, which may cancel out the benefits. Some critics also worry that squeezing the same work into fewer days could increase pressure rather than reduce it.

There is also the question of customers. A small business that is closed one extra day a week might lose clients to a competitor who is open. Some companies solve this by giving employees different days off, so the business stays open five days while each person works four. This works well in some teams and creates scheduling headaches in others.

What both sides tend to agree on is that time at work is not the same as useful work. Whether or not the four-day week becomes common, many organisations are already asking a valuable question: which of our working hours actually matter?', 'B2', 103),
  ('המחיר הנסתר של הנוחות', 'The Hidden Cost of Convenience', 'Few inventions have changed daily life as quietly as the delivery app. With a few taps, dinner, groceries or a phone charger arrive at the door, often within the hour. It is easy to see why so many of us have come to rely on them. What is harder to see, precisely because it is designed to be invisible, is what that convenience costs and who pays for it.

The most obvious cost is financial, and it is spread in ways customers rarely notice. Restaurants typically pay the platform a share of every order, and many respond by raising their menu prices on the app. The small delivery fee shown at checkout is therefore only part of the real price. The customer pays a little more, the restaurant earns a little less, and the platform takes its share from both.

Then there are the people doing the delivering. Couriers are usually classified as independent contractors rather than employees, which gives them flexibility but often removes the protections that come with ordinary employment, such as paid sick days. Their income depends on the number of orders they complete, which creates a constant incentive to hurry through traffic in all weather.

A less discussed cost is what happens to the street itself. When a neighbourhood''s shopping moves onto phones, the small shops that once gave the area its character can lose the passing customers they depend on. Some adapt by joining the platforms themselves; others close, and the street becomes quieter, even if just as many purchases are made.

None of this means that delivery apps are simply harmful. For people who are ill, elderly or overwhelmed by work, they can be genuinely valuable. The point is rather that convenience is never free. Each time we choose it, we are also choosing, without quite noticing, how a city''s work and money are distributed. Noticing is the first step towards choosing deliberately.', 'C1', 104),
  ('בשביל מה צריך ספריות היום', 'What Libraries Are For Now', 'When information became available on every phone, many people predicted that public libraries would slowly disappear. Why walk to a building full of books when almost any fact can be found in seconds? Yet in many towns, the local library is busier than ever, though not always for the reasons its founders imagined.

Part of the explanation is that a library was never only about access to information. It is one of the very few indoor public places where a person can stay for hours without being expected to buy anything. Students come to study in quiet, job seekers come to use computers and printers, parents bring small children to story time, and older people come for company as much as for books. In a city where almost every seat costs the price of a coffee, that is not a small thing.

Libraries have also quietly become places where people learn practical skills. Many run free workshops on filling in official forms online, recognising scam messages or using a new phone. These are not glamorous services, but for someone who has been left behind by a digital change, they can make the difference between managing alone and depending on others.

This shift has not been easy. Librarians are increasingly expected to act as teachers, technical support and, at times, social workers, often without the extra training or funding these roles require. Some critics argue that libraries are being asked to fill gaps that other public services should cover.

Still, the transformation suggests something important. The most valuable thing a library offers may no longer be its collection but its openness: a warm, free, shared space where anyone, for any reason, is allowed to be. In a time when so much of life happens privately on screens, that openness may matter more, not less.', 'C1', 105),
  ('על לטעות', 'On Being Wrong', 'There is a particular moment that most of us try to avoid: the moment of realising, mid-argument, that we are wrong. The feeling is so unpleasant that people go to considerable lengths to postpone it, reinterpreting evidence, shifting the terms of the debate, or simply changing the subject. What is curious is that being wrong, in itself, feels like nothing at all. Until we discover our mistake, a false belief feels exactly like a true one.

This asymmetry has uncomfortable implications. It means that the sense of certainty we bring to our views tells us very little about whether they are correct. Conviction is a feeling, not a measurement. People have been entirely certain about the shape of the earth, the causes of disease and the abilities of their neighbours, and entirely mistaken, without any internal signal to warn them.

If that is so, then the habit worth cultivating is not certainty but a kind of structured doubt: the willingness to ask, of any belief one holds strongly, what evidence would change one''s mind. A person who cannot answer that question is not necessarily wrong, but they are holding the belief in a way that no evidence could reach. That is less a position than a fortress.

None of this argues for endless hesitation. Decisions must be made, and a person who doubts everything equally is as unreliable as one who doubts nothing. The skill lies in calibration: holding each belief with roughly the confidence the evidence supports, and adjusting when the evidence moves.

Perhaps the most difficult part is social rather than intellectual. Admitting error in public can feel like a loss of status, and many environments, from politics to the workplace, reward those who never back down. Yet the people we tend to trust most, over time, are often those who say plainly, "I was wrong about that." Their admissions are what give their confidence its value.', 'C2', 106),
  ('המפה אינה השטח', 'The Map Is Not the Territory', '"The map is not the territory" is one of those phrases that sounds obvious until one tries to live by it. Nobody confuses a paper map with the landscape it describes. Yet in subtler forms, the confusion is everywhere: we routinely mistake our descriptions of the world for the world itself, and then are surprised when the world refuses to cooperate.

Every useful map is useful precisely because it leaves things out. A subway map that showed every curve of every tunnel would be accurate and useless; its value lies in its ruthless simplification. The same is true of the mental and statistical models we rely on. A budget, a job description, a medical guideline or an economic forecast each captures a few features of reality and ignores the rest. The danger arises not from the simplification, which is unavoidable, but from forgetting that it has taken place.

Consider the familiar case of targets. An organisation wishing to improve a hospital measures waiting times and rewards departments that reduce them. Before long, waiting times fall, but partly because patients are reclassified, or because the clock starts later than it used to. The number, which was meant to describe the territory, has become a territory of its own, to be managed for its own sake.

The remedy is not to abandon maps. Without them, we would be overwhelmed by detail and unable to act at all. The remedy is a certain habit of attention: periodically stepping away from the model to look directly at the thing it represents. A manager who reads reports should also walk the floor; a policymaker who studies indicators should also listen to the people those indicators count.

Maps are among the most powerful tools we have. But their power depends on our remembering, especially when they are most convincing, that they were drawn by someone, for some purpose, and that the land itself may have changed since.', 'C2', 107);

